{:edb/db-before-t 1215 :edb/db-after-t 1216 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534528 50 #inst "2026-10-07T20:29:07.168Z" 13194139534528 true] [17592186110508 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293263 13194139534528 true] [17592186110508 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293270 13194139534528 true] [17592186110510 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293256 13194139534528 true] [17592186111249 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534528 true] [17592186111249 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/a8a85024d555378ad7ba31262a290386777fa94b00208d6a68fdacb5bb61d3c8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" 13194139534528 true] [17592186111249 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/q-54482.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y = x^{3} - x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" 13194139534528 false] [17592186111249 1145 "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 1$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-1,1],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139534528 true] [17592186111251 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534528 true] [17592186111251 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y = 3x + 3x^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x =-2$ to $x = 0$." 13194139534528 false] [17592186111251 1100 "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3x+3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$" 13194139534528 true] [17592186111251 1145 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x(1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ and $x=0$ are our real solutions. Since $-1\\in [-2,0]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/16a25fdc08ed6db39932508fd829dc5f855b38ff9ec85083a08d05fed0d985ab.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,-1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}x\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{-2}^{-1}+{x}^{3}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(-1)^{2}-(-2)^{2}]+[(-1)^{3}-(-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+7 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{-1}^{0}-{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[0-(-1)^{2}]-[0-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=3.\n$$" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293256 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293257 13194139534528 true] [17592186293256 1089 "q-140573" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293256 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534528 true] [17592186293256 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/27bb4ee622f0b80e4b1a6edfae2c42f0676cafd0f9ad6150b979b32dbdabfc62.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293256 1113 #uuid "c0f8da35-3e73-511d-863e-fb9605a16342" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293256 1145 "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2-x)(2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 2$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-2,2],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-2,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293258 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293259 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293260 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293261 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293262 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1041 "selection" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293261 13194139534528 true] [17592186293257 1110 #uuid "e0b954d8-703b-5f17-861d-01c5d31c157b" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293258 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293258 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293258 1111 #uuid "997fa14e-e274-5fe7-be4b-5610a116c67f" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293259 1034 "{\\int }_{-4}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293259 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293259 1111 #uuid "f06d164d-719f-5c1c-8cf0-a0522d529f56" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293260 1034 "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293260 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293260 1111 #uuid "718aa89a-1e3c-50fc-8d11-8b0fd1e910ef" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293261 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293261 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293261 1111 #uuid "0b42f7d7-6be9-59c0-9c20-09c868147bcc" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293262 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293262 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293262 1111 #uuid "9fa61a86-fa52-5f24-861c-2f11860db70b" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293263 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293264 13194139534528 true] [17592186293263 1089 "q-54476" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293263 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534528 true] [17592186293263 1100 "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=4{x}^{3}+4$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293263 1113 #uuid "20edd780-8f94-530f-99ac-d687f3a1c198" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293263 1145 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is our only real solution. Since $-1\\in [-2,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/b0f2899bf6a946f6d7bf8bc60c41827bd05aef500f0fc0f30a5916c26b1007bb.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-4x{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[(-1)^{4}-(-2)^{4}]-4[-1+2] \\\\  & =-(-15)-4 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+4x{|}_{-1}^{-1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{4}-(-1)^{4}]+4[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=11+8=19.\n$$" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293265 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293266 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293267 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293268 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293269 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1041 "selection" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293267 13194139534528 true] [17592186293264 1110 #uuid "53b62923-b86b-50a8-b103-185ef2ff691f" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293265 1034 "20" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293265 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293265 1111 #uuid "5db9528f-2cd5-59dd-80e0-1ea3fe0cb9ba" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293266 1034 "11" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293266 1111 #uuid "840d1d60-7290-5b0c-a41c-6d0f08271d2a" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293267 1034 "19" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293267 1111 #uuid "0c2f8fdb-50fb-53b1-90f2-65dde0305b5a" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293268 1034 "3" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293268 1111 #uuid "c6a28015-5437-5ba5-83cd-8e31a8e550da" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293269 1034 "8" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534528 true] [17592186293269 1111 #uuid "49a0a506-a2f3-51dc-ab33-e3ab024ca168" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293270 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293271 13194139534528 true] [17592186293270 1089 "q-54494" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293270 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534528 true] [17592186293270 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/47e15993f3957fb3c1d7619fcf62544b2d5b358715538e17b9232dec5a0172c7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2$, as shown above." 13194139534528 true] [17592186293270 1113 #uuid "eaa58bcd-5c50-5bca-9be6-138888c6899f" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293270 1145 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=2$ is $x=1.$\n\nSince $1\\in [0,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =([\\frac{32}{5}-2]-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{26}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}=6.\n$$" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293272 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293273 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293274 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293275 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293276 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1041 "selection" 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293274 13194139534528 true] [17592186293271 1110 #uuid "c5cf1c0c-1eb5-564b-abf3-2c8a22d0c